+### Trusted setup status
+
+**The phase-2 trusted setup shipped in this repository is a demo setup. It is not
+a public ceremony, and no security claim rests on it.** We say this up front
+rather than waiting to be asked.
+
+Groth16 needs a two-phase setup. The two phases are in very different shape here:
+
+| Phase | What was used | Standing |
+|---|---|---|
+| Phase 1 (universal) | `powersOfTau28_hez_final_14.ptau` — Polygon Hermez perpetual Powers of Tau, 54 contributors plus a public beacon | Sound. Reused as-is, nothing to redo. |
+| Phase 2 (circuit-specific) | One contribution, named `aperture-mpp-1777587329`, with no beacon | **Demo only.** |
+
+A phase-2 setup is sound as long as at least one contributor destroyed their
+toxic waste. With one contribution and no beacon, that reduces to a single
+machine. Whoever held that entropy can forge a proof for **any** statement,
+including `is_compliant = 1` for a payment that violates the policy. The proofs
+still verify and the circuit still enforces what it says it enforces — but the
+soundness of the deployment rests on one party, not on nobody.
+
+Check the shipped artifact yourself:
+
+```bash
+node ../mandate/circuits/scripts/inspect-zkey-setup.mjs services/prover-service/artifacts/payment.zkey
+# phase-2 contributions    1
+# beacon applied           no
+```
+
+(`inspect-zkey-setup.mjs` lives in the
+[mandate](https://github.com/wienerlabs/mandate) repository, checked out beside
+this one; it reads the contribution list straight out of the zkey binary.)
+
+A public multi-party phase-2 ceremony is planned: contributions collected in the
+open, a beacon whose source is announced in advance, and published transcripts
+anyone can replay. Until that ceremony completes and its verifying key is
+deployed, this setup is described as a demo everywhere it is mentioned.
+
 ### Why Circom + snarkjs

 Circom + snarkjs Groth16 is the only ZK stack that targets BN254 natively, has on-chain verification primitives in Solana mainline (`alt_bn128_*` syscalls + groth16-solana), produces proofs small enough to submit in a single Solana transaction, and proves fast enough on commodity CPUs to run inside an HTTP service. Aperture previously evaluated zkVM stacks; the trade off (multi-second proving, ~250 KB receipts, additional verifier router contracts) was not justified for a payment-gating circuit of this size.
